<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-2-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li> Understand basics of first-order  differential equations</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p> This section introduces the concept of the first-order  differential equations and their applications. We will explore the general form of these equations, discuss their properties, and provide examples to illustrate their use in various contexts. </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-General-Form-First-Order-Equations">
    <title>General Form of First-Order Equations</title>
    <p> The general form of a first-order differential equation is given by: </p>
    <md>
      \frac{dy}{dx} = f(x, y)
    </md>
    <p> where <m>f(x, y)</m> is a function of two variables. </p>
  </subsection>

</section>